\title{Margins, Not Angles:\\
Decision--Vector Dissociation in the Shared Core\\
and Personal Tail of Fine-Tuned Transformers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the internal relationship between a pretrained transformer and a
fine-tune of it, and give a complete structural account of three phenomena
observed when comparing their layerwise attention caches: (i) the bottom of the
stack is \emph{exactly} shared, (ii) the layerwise divergence between the two
models is hump-shaped rather than monotone, rising to a peak around two-thirds
depth and then falling, and (iii) in the final two layers the two models' top-1
attention decisions agree only about $57\%$--$63\%$ of the time even though
their cached key vectors have cosine similarity $0.983$--$0.988$.

The third phenomenon is the sharpest. We prove that it is not an anomaly but a
theorem about score vectors. The correct stability certificate for a top-1
decision is a \emph{margin}: if a score vector $u$ holds its top choice by a gap
exceeding $2\eps$ and a second vector $v$ agrees with $u$ to within $\eps$ in
every coordinate, then $v$ makes the same choice; and the constant $2$ is sharp.
By contrast, \emph{no} function of cosine similarity alone can lower-bound
decision agreement: for every $\eps>0$ there are two score vectors of cosine
similarity greater than $1-\eps$ that decide differently. We call this the
\emph{decision--vector dissociation}.

We then explain \emph{where} the dissociation bites. Bounding the top attention
weight by the square root of the collision mass $\sum_k p_k^2$ shows that
diffuse attention necessarily has a small margin, hence is flippable by an
arbitrarily small perturbation. Independently, we prove a two-sided bridge
between the margin and the Maslov gap $\log\sum_j e^{x_j}-x_i$---the standard
measure of how far a softmax is from a tropical (max-plus) operation---so that
``far from tropical'', ``diffuse'' and ``decision-fragile'' are three names for
one inequality.

The hump receives an equally clean explanation. Under the recursion
$d_{k+1}\le L_kd_k+\eps$ governing divergence propagation, any downward step
exceeding the injected delta \emph{certifies} that the shared layer map
contracts. The measured constants force a quantitative dichotomy: either the
tail contracts by a factor of at most $4/5$, or the tail carries a total delta
budget of at least $0.057$, forcing some single layer to inject at least
$0.008$.

Finally we develop the engineering consequence. Serving $n$ fine-tunes with $s$
of $L$ layers shared costs $s+n(L-s)$, with amortized per-model ratio tending to
the tail fraction $(L-s)/L$---equal to $1/12$ for $22$ of $24$ layers---while a
margin certificate on the shared layers guarantees a provable decision agreement
fraction of at least $11/12$. An error-budget recursion yields a depth law: with
nonexpansive layers, per-layer delta $\eps$ and margin $m$, every layer of depth
$k<m/(2\eps)$ is certifiably shareable; and if the layers do not contract and
the margin profile does not increase with depth, the shareable layers form an
initial segment---sharing is a prefix property, exactly the empirical
``core shared, tail personal'' shape.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} attention margin, decision stability, cosine
similarity, Maslov dequantization, tropical geometry, collision mass,
key--value cache sharing, fine-tuning delta, contraction certificate.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Fine-tuning modifies every weight of a pretrained transformer, usually by a
small amount. A natural mental model of what this does to the network's internal
state is \emph{cumulative drift}: perturbations injected at each layer propagate
upward and compound, so the distance between the base model's activations and
the fine-tune's activations should increase monotonically with depth.

Measurement contradicts this picture twice over. On a $24$-layer transformer
compared against an instruction-tuned version of itself, on identical held-out
prompts:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Layer-$0$ keys are exactly identical} (cosine similarity
        $1.0000$).
  \item \textbf{Every layer keeps cosine similarity $\ge 0.976$} for keys (mean
        $0.990$); values sit in $0.94$--$0.99$.
  \item \textbf{The divergence is a hump.} Relative key divergence climbs to
        $0.217$ at layer $16$ and falls back to about $0.16$; hidden-state
        divergence peaks near $0.22$ at layers $12$--$16$.
  \item \textbf{Mean top-1 attention decision agreement is $0.894$} across
        layers, but collapses to $0.568$ at layer $22$ and $0.627$ at layer
        $23$---while those same layers' key cosine similarities are $0.983$ and
        $0.988$.
\end{itemize}

The last bullet is the crux: \emph{vector similarity does not bound functional
divergence}.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The correct certificate is a margin (Theorem~\ref{thm:margin}), and the
        constant $2$ is sharp (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}).
  \item Cosine similarity certifies nothing (Theorem~\ref{thm:dissoc}).
  \item Diffuse attention is decision-fragile
        (Theorems~\ref{thm:collision}--\ref{thm:fragile}).
  \item A two-sided margin/Maslov-gap bridge
        (Theorems~\ref{thm:margin2trop}--\ref{thm:smallflip},
        Corollary~\ref{cor:fartropfragile}).
  \item The hump is a contraction certificate (Theorem~\ref{thm:contract}) with
        an explicit numerical dichotomy
        (Corollaries~\ref{cor:factor}, \ref{cor:budget}).
  \item A sharing budget and a tight depth law (Section~\ref{sec:budget}).
  \item A serving law and a causal prediction
        (Sections~\ref{sec:serving}--\ref{sec:swap}).
\end{enumerate}

Every bound below is explicit and finitary; nothing is asymptotic except the
serving limit of Theorem~\ref{thm:amortized}.

\section{Setup and definitions}

Fix $n\ge1$ and work with real \emph{score vectors} $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, the
pre-softmax attention logits of one layer over $n$ candidate positions.

\begin{definition}[Decision]
An index $i$ is the \emph{strict top} (the \emph{decision}) of $u$, written
$\Top(u)=i$, if $u_j<u_i$ for all $j\ne i$. We say $u$ has \emph{no decision} if
no index is a strict top.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Margin]
The \emph{margin} of $u$ at $i$ is $\min_{j\ne i}(u_i-u_j)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform perturbation]
Two vectors are \emph{$\eps$-close} if $\|u-v\|_\infty\le\eps$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cosine similarity]
For nonzero $u,v$,
$\cos(u,v)=\langle u,v\rangle/(\|u\|_2\|v\|_2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Collision mass]
For nonnegative $p$, $C(p)=\sum_kp_k^2$. For a distribution this is the
probability that two independent draws coincide; it equals $1$ for a point mass
and $1/m$ for the uniform distribution on $m$ atoms.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Maslov gap]
With $\lse(x)=\log\sum_je^{x_j}$, the \emph{Maslov gap} of $x$ at $i$ is
$\gamma(x,i)=\lse(x)-x_i$. It measures the failure of softmax to be a hard max,
i.e.\ the distance from tropical (max-plus) arithmetic.
\end{definition}

\section{The correct stability certificate is a margin}

\begin{theorem}[Margin stability]\label{thm:margin}
Let $u,v\in\mathbb{R}^n$, let $i$ be an index and let $\eps\in\mathbb{R}$.
Suppose $u_i-u_j>2\eps$ for all $j\ne i$ and $|u_j-v_j|\le\eps$ for all $j$.
Then $\Top(v)=i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $j\ne i$. Then $v_j\le u_j+\eps$ and $v_i\ge u_i-\eps$, so
$v_i-v_j\ge(u_i-u_j)-2\eps>0$.
\end{proof}

The factor $2$ arises because a perturbation may simultaneously depress the
leader and elevate a challenger. It cannot be improved.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the factor $2$]\label{thm:sharp}
For every $\eps>0$ there exist $u,v\in\mathbb{R}^2$ with $u_0-u_1=2\eps$
exactly, $|u_j-v_j|\le\eps$ for both $j$, $\Top(u)=0$, and $v$ has no decision.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $u=(2\eps,0)$ and $v=(\eps,\eps)$. The gap of $u$ is exactly $2\eps$, each
coordinate moves by exactly $\eps$, and $v$ ties.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[All-layer agreement from a uniform margin]
Let $u^{(l)},v^{(l)}$ be the per-layer score vectors of two models and $i^{(l)}$
indices. If $u^{(l)}_{i^{(l)}}-u^{(l)}_j>2\eps$ for all $j\ne i^{(l)}$ and all
$l$, and $\|u^{(l)}-v^{(l)}\|_\infty\le\eps$, then both models decide $i^{(l)}$
at every layer $l$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:margin} layerwise gives the claim for $v$; the hypothesis
$|u^{(l)}_j-v^{(l)}_j|\le\eps$ forces $\eps\ge0$, whence
$u^{(l)}_{i^{(l)}}>u^{(l)}_j$.
\end{proof}

\section{Cosine similarity certifies nothing}

\begin{definition}[The flip pair]
For $t>0$ set $u^{(t)}=(1+t,\,1)$ and $v^{(t)}=(1,\,1+t)$ in $\mathbb{R}^2$;
then $\Top(u^{(t)})=0$ and $\Top(v^{(t)})=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Cosine of the flip pair]\label{lem:flipcos}
For all $t\in\mathbb{R}$,
\[
\cos\!\big(u^{(t)},v^{(t)}\big)=\frac{2+2t}{t^2+2t+2}
=1-\frac{t^2}{t^2+2t+2},
\]
and for $t>0$ one has $\cos(u^{(t)},v^{(t)})\ge 1-t/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The inner product is $(1+t)+(1+t)=2+2t$ and each squared norm is
$(1+t)^2+1=t^2+2t+2$, so the product of the norms is $t^2+2t+2$; the second
form follows from $(t^2+2t+2)-(2+2t)=t^2$. For $t>0$ we have $t^2+2t+2>2t>0$,
so $1-\cos=t^2/(t^2+2t+2)<t^2/(2t)=t/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Decision--vector dissociation]\label{thm:dissoc}
For every $\eps>0$ there exist $u,v\in\mathbb{R}^2$ with
$\cos(u,v)>1-\eps$, $\Top(u)=0$ and $\Top(v)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $t=\eps$, $u=u^{(t)}$, $v=v^{(t)}$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:flipcos},
$\cos(u,v)\ge1-\eps/2>1-\eps$, while the decisions differ.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No cosine certificate exists]
There is no function $\Phi:[-1,1]\to[0,1]$ with $\Phi(c)\to1$ as $c\to1$ such
that decision agreement is bounded below by $\Phi(\cos(u,v))$. In particular the
observed tail configuration---cosine $0.983$ with agreement $0.568$---is
structurally possible and refutes no inequality.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
In the flip pair the coordinatewise perturbation is exactly $t$ and the top-1
gap of $u^{(t)}$ is also exactly $t$. Since $t\not>2t$ for $t>0$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:margin} does not apply: the flip occurs precisely in the region
the margin certificate declines to cover. Cosine similarity is a statement about
direction; a decision is a statement about order, and small angles do not
control order near a tie.
\end{remark}

\section{Why the diffuse tail is the fragile region}

\begin{theorem}[Collision bound on the top weight]\label{thm:collision}
Let $p\in\mathbb{R}^n$ be nonnegative. Then $p_i\le\sqrt{C(p)}$ for every $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$p_i^2$ is one summand of the nonnegative sum $\sum_kp_k^2=C(p)$; take square
roots using $p_i\ge0$.
\end{proof}

For an attention distribution, $1/C(p)$ is the participation ratio---the
effective number of positions attended to---so the leader's weight never exceeds
the reciprocal square root of the effective support size.

\begin{theorem}[Diffuse attention is decision-fragile]\label{thm:fragile}
Let $p$ be nonnegative with $\Top(p)=i$, let $j\ne i$, and let $\eta>0$. Then
there exists $q$ with $\|p-q\|_\infty\le\sqrt{C(p)}+\eta$ and $\Top(q)=j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Set $q=p$ except $q_j=p_i+\eta$. Only coordinate $j$ moves, and there
$|p_j-q_j|=p_i+\eta-p_j\le p_i+\eta\le\sqrt{C(p)}+\eta$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} and $p_j\ge0$. For $k\ne j$ we have
$q_k=p_k\le p_i<q_j$.
\end{proof}

The perturbation needed is of size $\sqrt{C(p)}$, and the decision may be moved
to \emph{any} chosen index. In the diffuse tail of the measured network this is
comfortably below the measured relative fine-tune delta of about $0.16$.

\section{The tropical bridge: Maslov gap versus margin}

Softmax is Maslov dequantization of the max-plus semiring. A separate
measurement on the same stack found the Maslov gap near $0$ at almost every
layer, jumping to a median of $2.5$--$2.7$ exactly at layers $22$ and $23$. We
show this is the same fact as the margin collapse.

\begin{lemma}[Range of the gap]
For all $x$ and $i$, $\gamma(x,i)\ge0$; and if $i$ maximises $x$ then
$\gamma(x,i)\le\log n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$e^{x_i}\le\sum_je^{x_j}\le ne^{x_i}$ in the maximal case; apply $\log$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Margin implies near-tropical]\label{thm:margin2trop}
If $x_i-x_j\ge m$ for all $j\ne i$, then
$\gamma(x,i)\le\log\!\big(1+(n-1)e^{-m}\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Split $\sum_je^{x_j}=e^{x_i}+\sum_{j\ne i}e^{x_j}$; each of the $n-1$ tail terms
is at most $e^{x_i}e^{-m}$, so the sum is at most
$e^{x_i}(1+(n-1)e^{-m})$. Take logarithms and subtract $x_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Far from tropical implies small margin]\label{thm:trop2margin}
Suppose $n\ge2$, $x_i-x_j\ge m$ for all $j\ne i$, and $\gamma(x,i)\ge g>0$. Then
$m\le\log(n-1)-\log(e^g-1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:margin2trop}, $g\le\log(1+(n-1)e^{-m})$; exponentiate to get
$(e^g-1)/(n-1)\le e^{-m}$ and take logarithms, using $e^g-1>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[One nat of gap costs one nat of margin]\label{cor:natcost}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:trop2margin} with $g\ge1$,
$m\le\log(n-1)+\log2-g$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$g\ge1>\log2$ gives $e^g\ge2$, hence $e^g-1\ge e^g/2$ and
$\log(e^g-1)\ge g-\log2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Small margin implies flippable]\label{thm:smallflip}
Suppose $j\ne i$, $x_i-x_j\le m$ with $m\ge0$, and $\eta>0$. Then there is $y$
with $\|x-y\|_\infty\le(m+\eta)/2$ and $y_i<y_j$; in particular
$\Top(y)\ne i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $d=(m+\eta)/2$ and take $y=x$ except $y_i=x_i-d$, $y_j=x_j+d$. Then
$y_j-y_i=(x_j-x_i)+2d\ge\eta>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Far from tropical implies fragile]\label{cor:fartropfragile}
Let $n\ge2$, $g\ge1$, $\delta,\eta>0$, $\gamma(x,i)\ge g$ and
$M:=\log(n-1)+\log2-g+\delta\ge0$. Then there are $j\ne i$ and $y$ with
$\|x-y\|_\infty\le(M+\eta)/2$ and $y_i<y_j$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If every $j\ne i$ satisfied $x_i-x_j\ge M$, Corollary~\ref{cor:natcost} with
margin $M$ would give $M\le M-\delta$. Choose a competitor within $M$ of the top
and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:smallflip}.
\end{proof}

Thus large margin $\iff$ near-tropical $\iff$ decision-stable, and small margin
$\iff$ far-from-tropical $\iff$ decision-fragile. Three independent
diagnostics---a tropical one, an information-theoretic one and a behavioural
one---must single out the same layers.

\section{The hump is a contraction certificate}

Let both networks read the same input and run through the \emph{same} layer maps
$f_k$ on a normed space $E$, the fine-tune injecting a per-layer delta:
\[
a_{k+1}=f_k(a_k),\qquad b_{k+1}=f_k(b_k)+\delta_k,\qquad d_k:=\|a_k-b_k\|.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Exactly shared prefix]
If $a_0=b_0$ and $\delta_k=0$ for all $k<s$, then $a_k=b_k$ for all $k\le s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction: $a_{k+1}=f_k(a_k)=f_k(b_k)=b_{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric and linear bounds]\label{thm:geom}
If $d_0=0$, $L\ge0$ and $d_{k+1}\le Ld_k+\eps$, then
$d_k\le\eps\sum_{i<k}L^i$; for nonexpansive layers ($L=1$),
$d_k\le k\eps$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$, using $\sum_{i<k+1}L^i=L\sum_{i<k}L^i+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Downward steps certify contraction]\label{thm:contract}
Let $\|\delta\|\le\eps$ and suppose
$\|f(a)-(f(b)+\delta)\|<\|a-b\|-\eps$. Then $\|f(a)-f(b)\|<\|a-b\|$: the shared
layer map strictly contracts the pair $(a,b)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $f(a)-f(b)=(f(a)-(f(b)+\delta))+\delta$ and use the triangle inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative contraction factor]\label{thm:factor}
If $\|\delta\|\le\eps$ and $\|f(a)-(f(b)+\delta)\|+\eps\le c\|a-b\|$, then
$\|f(a)-f(b)\|\le c\|a-b\|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The measured contraction factor]\label{cor:factor}
With $\|a-b\|=0.217$, $\|f(a)-(f(b)+\delta)\|\le0.16$ and $\eps=0.01$, the
shared tail map satisfies $\|f(a)-f(b)\|\le\tfrac45\|a-b\|$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$0.16+0.01=0.17\le0.8\times0.217=0.1736$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:factor}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chain lower bound]\label{thm:chain}
If $d_k-e_k\le d_{k+1}$ for all $k$, then $d_m-\sum_{k=m}^{n-1}e_k\le d_n$ for
$m\le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Delta budget of the descending stretch]\label{cor:budget}
If $d_{16}=0.217$ and $d_{23}=0.16$, then $\sum_{k=16}^{22}e_k\ge0.057$, and
consequently some single layer $k\in[16,23)$ has $e_k\ge0.008$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:chain} with $m=16$, $n=23$; if all seven terms were below
$0.008$ their sum would be below $0.056<0.057$.
\end{proof}

Monotone divergence was never an axiom to be refuted empirically: its failure is
\emph{exactly} contractivity of the shared machinery, and the measured hump
produces the dichotomy above.

\section{The margin--depth sharing budget}\label{sec:budget}

\begin{definition}[Error budget]
Given $L_k\ge0$ and $\eps\ge0$, set $B_0=0$ and $B_{k+1}=L_kB_k+\eps$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The budget dominates the divergence]\label{thm:budget}
If $d_0=0$ and $d_{k+1}\le L_kd_k+\eps$ with $L_k\ge0$, then $d_k\le B_k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Linear and monotone budgets]
If $\eps\ge0$ and $0\le L_k\le1$, then $B_k\le k\eps$, with equality when
$L_k\equiv1$. If instead $L_k\ge1$ for all $k$, then $k\mapsto B_k$ is
non-decreasing.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Shareable layer]
Given a margin profile $m_k$, layer $k$ is \emph{certifiably shareable} if
$2B_k<m_k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Agreement on shareable layers]
If the reference model's layer-$k$ scores have margin at least $m_k$ at $i_k$,
the two models' layer-$k$ scores are $d_k$-close coordinatewise, the divergence
obeys the recursion of Theorem~\ref{thm:budget}, and layer $k$ is shareable, then
the second model also decides $i_k$ at layer $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$d_k\le B_k$ and $m_k>2B_k$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:margin} with $\eps=B_k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharing is a prefix property]\label{thm:prefix}
Assume $\eps\ge0$, $L_k\ge1$ for all $k$, and a non-increasing margin profile
($m_l\le m_k$ whenever $k\le l$). If layer $l$ is shareable and $k\le l$, then
layer $k$ is shareable; the shareable layers form an initial segment.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$2B_k\le2B_l<m_l\le m_k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Depth law]\label{thm:depth}
For nonexpansive layers and $\eps>0$, every layer of depth $k<m_k/(2\eps)$ is
certifiably shareable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The measured configuration, and its tightness]
With $\eps=0.01$, uniform margin $m=0.5$ and nonexpansive layers, every layer of
depth $k\le24$ is certifiably shareable. The bound is tight: for $L_k\equiv1$ the
certificate fails at depth $25$, since $B_{25}=0.25$ and
$2\times0.25=0.5\not<0.5$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The serving law}\label{sec:serving}

\begin{definition}[Serving cost]
Serving $n$ fine-tunes of an $L$-layer model with $s$ layers of key/value
machinery shared costs $\cost(n,L,s)=s+n(L-s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Saving]
$\cost(n,L,s)=nL-(n-1)s$; sharing saves exactly $(n-1)s$, and for $n\ge2$,
$s>0$ we have $\cost(n,L,s)<nL$ strictly.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Amortized model-delta law]\label{thm:amortized}
For $L>0$, $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\cost(n,L,s)}{nL}=\frac{L-s}{L}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\dfrac{s+n(L-s)}{nL}=\dfrac{s/L}{n}+\dfrac{L-s}{L}$ and the first term tends to
$0$.
\end{proof}

For $L=24$, $s=22$ the limit is $1/12$: the shared core amortizes away entirely
and only the personal tail is paid for.

\begin{theorem}[Shared-core safety]\label{thm:safety}
Let $S$ be a set of layers on which the reference model has margin exceeding
$2\eps$ and the shared cache is $\eps$-accurate coordinatewise. Then both models
decide identically on every layer of $S$, so the provable agreement fraction is
at least $|S|/L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Certifying $22$ layers out of $24$ yields a provable agreement fraction of at
least $11/12\approx0.9167$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The convenient slogan ``share $22$ of $24$ layers at $\ge0.92$ agreement''
conflates two numbers: $22/24=0.91\overline{6}<0.92$. The certified figure is
exactly $11/12$.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[No cosine certificate licenses sharing]
For every $\eps>0$ there exist two layers' score vectors with cosine similarity
above $1-\eps$ whose top-1 decisions differ.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:dissoc}.
\end{proof}

Hence the safety hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:safety} must be a margin, which
is exactly why the measured tail is not shareable while the core is.

\section{Tail-swap attribution: a prediction}\label{sec:swap}

Factor each model as $\mathrm{tail}\circ\mathrm{core}$, with
$\mathrm{core}:E\to E$ mapping the input to the state at the splitting depth.

\begin{theorem}[Divergence splitting]
If $\mathrm{tail}_A$ is $K$-Lipschitz, then for every input $x$,
\[
\|\mathrm{tail}_A(\mathrm{core}_Ax)-\mathrm{tail}_B(\mathrm{core}_Bx)\|
\le K\|\mathrm{core}_Ax-\mathrm{core}_Bx\|
+\|\mathrm{tail}_A(\mathrm{core}_Bx)-\mathrm{tail}_B(\mathrm{core}_Bx)\|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Attribution]
If $K\ge0$, $\|\mathrm{core}_Ax-\mathrm{core}_Bx\|\le\eps$, and the observed
output divergence is at least $D$, then
$\|\mathrm{tail}_A(\mathrm{core}_Bx)-\mathrm{tail}_B(\mathrm{core}_Bx)\|\ge
D-K\eps$. Identity measured at the output and absent from the core must live in
the tail.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Predicted outcome of the causal swap]
Suppose $\|\mathrm{core}_Bx-\mathrm{core}_Ax\|\le\eps$, that $\mathrm{tail}_B$
is coordinatewise $K$-Lipschitz, and that model $B$ holds its decision $i$ with
margin exceeding $2K\eps$ on its own core state. Then the hybrid model
$\mathrm{tail}_B\circ\mathrm{core}_A$ makes exactly model $B$'s decision $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The score vectors $\mathrm{tail}_B(\mathrm{core}_Bx)$ and
$\mathrm{tail}_B(\mathrm{core}_Ax)$ are $K\eps$-close coordinatewise; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:margin}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:dissoc}, dropping the margin hypothesis invalidates the
conclusion: ``the caches look alike'' never licenses a swap.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Margin certification of a shared core]
Given per-layer reference score vectors and cache accuracies $\eps_k$, compute
$m_k=u^{(k)}_{i_k}-\max_{j\ne i_k}u^{(k)}_j$ and mark layer $k$ shareable iff
$m_k>2\eps_k$. Every marked layer provably reproduces the reference decision.
Cost $O(Ln)$, one pass, no model re-evaluation.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Depth-budget planner]
Given $L_k$, $\eps$ and $m_k$, iterate $B_{k+1}=L_kB_k+\eps$ from $B_0=0$ and
return the largest $s$ with $2B_k<m_k$ for all $k<s$. Under the hypotheses of
Theorem~\ref{thm:prefix} this is exactly the shareable prefix. Cost $O(L)$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Hump dichotomy resolver]
Given a divergence profile $d_k$ and delta budget $\eps$, report each index with
$d_{k+1}<d_k-\eps$ as a contraction certificate with factor at most
$(d_{k+1}+\eps)/d_k$, and over each descending stretch $[m,n)$ report the forced
total delta budget $d_m-d_n$ and the pigeonhole per-layer bound
$(d_m-d_n)/(n-m)$. Cost $O(L)$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\section{Discussion}

The theorems here are unconditional statements about score vectors, divergence
recursions and serving costs. The measurements that motivated them come from one
base/fine-tune pair, one context and a small prompt set at half precision. What
the theory contributes is the assurance that the striking observation---cosine
$0.983$ with agreement $0.568$---needs no special explanation, and that the
correct instrument for the engineering question is a margin, computable in one
pass.

Conversely, the results are a warning. Cosine similarity of internal
representations is routinely used as a proxy for functional equivalence in model
merging, cache sharing, quantization evaluation and representational-similarity
analysis. Theorem~\ref{thm:dissoc} says this proxy has no worst-case content near
a decision boundary, and Theorem~\ref{thm:fragile} says the worst case is
realised precisely where attention is diffuse---empirically, the top of the
stack.

The most satisfying structural finding is the coincidence of three independent
diagnostics on the same two layers. Sections~5 and 6 show they bound each other
in both directions, so the coincidence is forced. The methodological conclusion
is: measure the margin, since it is what all three diagnostics approximate and
what appears in every stability theorem.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Causal tail swap.} The prediction of Section~\ref{sec:swap} is
falsifiable: exchanging only the final two layers between two fine-tunes should
transfer the decision, provided the tail margins exceed $2K\eps$.

\textbf{Scale and training regime.} Does the shared-core/personal-tail split hold
at larger scales, and does the transition depth depend on the alignment method?
The depth law predicts that methods with smaller per-layer deltas share deeper
cores.

\textbf{Precision allocation.} The depth law is symmetric in $m$ and $\eps$:
high-margin layers tolerate large $\eps$. This argues for quantizing the
high-margin core more aggressively than the low-margin tail.

\textbf{Beyond top-1.} A top-$k$ analogue needs the gap between the $k$-th and
$(k{+}1)$-st order statistics; the margin machinery should carry over, with the
collision bound replaced by a bound on the $k$-th largest coordinate.

\textbf{Prompt and context variance.} Characterising the distribution of
per-layer margins over a realistic prompt distribution would turn the worst-case
certificates here into average-case guarantees.

\section{Conclusion}

Decisions are order statistics, and order is controlled by margins, not angles.
The margin certificate is sharp, with optimal constant $2$; the cosine
certificate does not exist. Diffuse attention has a small margin by the collision
bound, and the Maslov gap measures the same diffuseness from the tropical side,
so ``far from tropical'', ``diffuse'' and ``fragile'' coincide. The divergence
hump is not a refutation of monotone drift but a certificate of contraction in
the shared machinery. And the whole picture cashes out as a design rule: share
the high-margin prefix, whose depth is the margin divided by twice the per-layer
delta; pay per-model for the low-margin tail; and never accept an angle where a
margin is required.

The bulk of a transformer is shared machinery. The identity is in the tail.

